2026-08-29T10:10Z claude note the update-cadence question is ANSWERED by measurement, not left open — see "The cadence question, settled" in the body. FTS5 does not give the high-df terms for free: the vocabulary scan costs ~3,4 s warm and 7,5 s cold over 639 888 terms, so the droplist is derived state and needs a home, but it is 23 terms and 53 bytes and the index build already costs 263,7 s, which makes the build the obvious and only recompute point

2026-08-29T10:25Z claude note cross-granularity control on the droplist, measured 2026-08-29 against Zotero 10's own FTS5 index (fulltext.sqlite, 13 090 documents, item-level): at the same 30% threshold that yields 23 terms on our 477 512-passage index, the platform index yields 777 — 34x more, because document frequency at item granularity is a different quantity from document frequency at passage granularity. Its top terms are the bare digits (1 in 11 873 of 13 090 docs, 2 in 11 755, 10 in 11 217) since every document carries page numbers, years and references. Importing a droplist from the platform would drop every numeral from every query. This is independent evidence FOR this ticket's central choice: corpus-derived means derived from THIS corpus at THIS granularity, and the ticket already gets that right. No design change implied. Two further notes: the scan is not cheaper on the platform side either (670 680 terms in 4,0 s there against 639 888 in 3,4 s here), so the meta-row-plus-drift cadence stays necessary; and fts5vocab reads term and doc counts happily off a CONTENTLESS table, so vocabulary statistics are available from the platform index even though its text is not (see 0120). Worth a cheap check while here: whether our own 23 terms include numerals, and how the degeneracy fallback behaves on a numeric query such as 2019 or a DOI fragment — passage granularity likely keeps digits well under threshold, but it is cheaper to check than to learn from a user.
--- body ---
## Context
The measured replacement for the stoplist deletion that failed X2 (ticket
0014). The author's proposal, swept on the real 477 512-passage index:
**drop query terms whose document frequency exceeds the DESIGN §3 threshold,
and fall back to the raw token set when fewer than two terms survive.**

Measured, direct-SQL, against the two arms the abandoned PR was choosing
between:

| policy | p50 | p95 | matches unfiltered | degenerate query |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| stoplist (upstream today) | 215,2 ms | 365,8 ms | 86% | broken, 10% |
| stoplist deleted (the held PR) | 1 055,3 ms | 1 211,7 ms | 100% | fixed |
| droplist + degeneracy fallback | 274,2 ms | 536,9 ms | 98% | fixed |

It dominates both. It is also the only form that is well posed against a
shared token space: a per-language stoplist would have to drop German `die`
while keeping English `die`, an intent the index cannot see, whereas asking
what a string costs in *this* corpus has one answer
(`multilingual-cost.json`).

## The cadence question, settled

FTS5 does **not** hand over the high-df terms for free, which was the open
question. `fts5vocab` is a virtual table over the existing index — no
migration, no rebuild, nothing stored — but a df threshold needs a full scan
of it, because the table is ordered by term and not by document count. On
the real index that scan costs **~3,4 s warm and 7,5 s cold over 639 888
terms**. Too slow for query time, and too slow to pay at every server start.

So the droplist is derived state and needs somewhere to live. Everything
else about it is small: at the 30% threshold it is **23 terms and 53 bytes**,
and `meta` (key/value) already exists in the schema and already holds
`schemaVersion` and PR-3's library stamp.

That fixes the cadence with no new machinery and no invalidation rule:

- **Recompute at the end of every full build.** The build already walks the
  whole corpus and costs 263,7 s; a 3,4 s scan is 1,3% on top, invisible.
- **Do not recompute on every incremental update.** A handful of new items
  cannot move a 30% threshold, and paying 3,4 s on a small update is the one
  way this becomes user-visible. Store the passage count alongside the
  droplist and rescan only when it has moved by more than ~10% since, so the
  cost is amortised against the drift that could actually change the list.
- **An index with no stored droplist behaves exactly as today**, filtering
  nothing, and adopts one on its first build. Same shape as PR-3's stamp
  adoption, and it means no existing index is stranded.

There is therefore no staleness question left for the author to rule on, and
no cadence knob to expose.

## Relevant files (upstream tree)
- `src/features/search/tokenize.ts` — holds the 29-word literal today.
  `tokenize()` must stay pure and language-agnostic; the droplist is a
  parameter, not a new global.
- `src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts` — `keyword()` at ~line 466 builds the
  MATCH string and already dedupes with `[...new Set(tokenize(q))]`; the
  `meta` helpers and `createSchema` are here.
- `src/features/search/bm25.ts` — `addDoc()` runs `tokenize()` over the
  DOCUMENT, so the memory backend must not silently inherit query-side
  filtering. Decide and test which side each policy applies to.
- `src/features/search/index-manager.ts` — `makeSnippet()` also tokenizes the
  query and centres on the earliest matching token, which is how the deletion
  broke snippets; whatever ships must be checked against that.

## Actions
1. Derive the droplist at the end of a build (and on the >10% drift
   condition) with one `fts5vocab` scan; persist terms plus the passage count
   in `meta`.
2. Apply it query-side only, after tokenize and before the MATCH string, with
   the degeneracy fallback at fewer than two surviving terms.
3. Delete the 29-word literal.
4. Decide the memory backend explicitly: it re-derives postings on load, and
   `addDoc` shares `tokenize()`, so state whether the droplist applies there
   and test the parity case either way.
5. Re-check snippets: with high-df terms dropped, `makeSnippet` should centre
   on content again — assert it.

## Test
Red first, on stock: a query of function words returns one incidental term
and unrelated hits. Then the fallback case, the parity case between backends,
the adoption case (no stored droplist filters nothing), and a snippet case.

## Verification
- [ ] p95 on the 477k index inside the DESIGN §3 budget, re-measured with
      `bench/query.py --repeat 6`, not asserted
- [ ] degenerate query returns the same hits as an unfiltered query
- [ ] an index built before this change filters nothing until rebuilt
- [ ] snippets centre on content, not on the passage opening
- [ ] upstream's own gates: typecheck, lint, full suite

## Invariants
- DESIGN.md §3 owns the threshold (0090). This ticket implements it and
  restates no number.
- `tokenize()` stays language-agnostic: no language detection, ever — the
  homograph measurement is why.
- No existing index is stranded or silently re-ranked without a rebuild.

## Exit criteria
- Implemented on the fork, green on upstream's gates, p95 re-measured inside
  budget, and an upstream slot free. **The slot is the real gate**: ticket
  0016's cross-library wipe guard fixes silent data loss and outranks this,
  and the Zotero-native baseline showed matching to be the lower-value half
  of the layer. Do not spend a slot here while 0016 is queued.
